export const lsConcepts601700: any[] = [
  { id: "ls-nama-601", title: "Darāndolitadīrghākṣī", category: "Hṛdaya-rājñī", summary: "She who has long, tremulous eyes. Literally dīrgha-akṣī praises eyes so long they nearly touch the ears, while dara-āndolita describes their gentle trembling rotation toward every quarter. The commentary reads this restlessness as maternal vigilance rather than distraction: the Universal Mother ceaselessly sweeps the worlds with Her glance to catch the cry of Her children and dispel their fear, which is why Mūka Pañcaśatī devotes a full hundred verses to Her eyes and Saundarya Laharī sings them as well. Like any mother who turns at the sound of her child, She turns toward whoever calls Mā. In practice, sit quietly, call inwardly, and feel those long compassionate eyes circling until they rest upon you, letting the sense of being seen melt fear.", relatedVerseIds: ["ls-121"] },
  { id: "ls-nama-602", title: "Darahāsojjvalanmukhī", category: "Hṛdaya-rājñī", summary: "She whose face is radiant with a smile. The name joins dara-hāsa, a soft restrained smile rather than open laughter, with ujjvala-mukhī, a face made luminous by it. Doctrinally this half-smile is the visible form of grace: eyes reveal compassion, but the smile distributes it, drawing even strangers toward devotion without argument or miracle, since mercy legible on a face converts hearts faster than doctrine. The commentary stresses that compassion is not an abstract attribute here but something seen, met, and answered like the warmth of a mother greeting her child. In practice, hold Her faintly smiling face before the mind at the start of meditation, and let your own face soften in reply until the boundary between Her smile and yours dissolves.", relatedVerseIds: ["ls-121"] },
  { id: "ls-nama-603", title: "Gurumūrtiḥ", category: "Hṛdaya-rājñī", summary: "She who has assumed a severe form or one who has assumed the form of the guru. Guru means the dispeller of darkness, and in a second sense one who embodies supreme knowledge of Brahman, so the name hails Her as the Supreme Guru rather than a stern figure. The commentary anchors this in Śrīvidyā non-dualism, citing Varivasyā-rahasya to the effect that Mother, mantra, cakra, guru, and disciple are to be realized as one identity, conferred only through the guru's grace, a rank Viṣṇu Sahasranāma also grants Viṣṇu. Hence initiation is recognition, not mere instruction. In practice, bow to teacher, mantra, and deity as a single presence, and receive every teaching as the Mother Herself dispelling your inner dark.", relatedVerseIds: ["ls-121"] },
  { id: "ls-nama-604", title: "Guṇanidhiḥ", category: "Hṛdaya-rājñī", summary: "She who is the treasure house of all good qualities. The name pictures every noble quality stored in Her like wealth in a treasury, since the three guṇas of Sāṁkhya in all their countless proportions arise from Her alone, exactly as Sāṁkhya-sūtra describes constituents agreeing and differing through lightness and the rest. The commentary widens guṇa to nine subtle endowments including kalā, kula, nāma, jñāna, cit, nāda, bindu, kalpa, and jīva, and recalls the charming story in which guṇa means rope: at the great deluge Devī Herself became the rope by which Viṣṇu as fish towed the boat of living seeds. In practice, survey any virtue you admire, trace it to its storehouse in Her, and ask Her to release it into your life.", relatedVerseIds: ["ls-121"] },
  { id: "ls-nama-605", title: "Gomātā", category: "Hṛdaya-rājñī", summary: "She who became surabhī, the cow that grants all wishes. Literally go-mātā is the Mother of cows, those sacred givers of nourishment, and esoterically She is kāmadhenu, the wish-yielding cow who feeds every need without exhaustion. The commentary traces the Vedic word go through its layered senses: in outer sacrifice it is the cow, while in inner sacrifice it is knowledge itself, and gauḥ further signifies speech, rays of light, and heaven, all shown streaming from Her as their single origin. Thus nurturing, knowing, and speaking are one maternal act. In practice, treat every meal, every true sentence, and every ray of understanding as milk from Her udder, and protect living sources of nourishment as Her body.", relatedVerseIds: ["ls-121"] },
  { id: "ls-nama-606", title: "Guhajanmabhūḥ", category: "Hṛdaya-rājñī", summary: "She who is the mother of guhā (subramaṇya). Guha means the hidden or concealed, and janma-bhūḥ the birthplace, so one reading makes Her the womb from which souls veiled by avidyā emerge, like Bṛhadāraṇyaka's sparks flying from a single fire, each soul a spark of Lalitāmbikā the Self. Read with the opening name Śrī Mātā, this perfectly restates the Upaniṣadic teaching that all beings emanate from Brahman. Yet guha is also a name of Kārttikeya, also called Subrahmaṇya and Skanda, the general of the gods praised in the Gītā, giving the second reading that She bore the war-god Himself. In practice, trace yourself backward from veiled spark to the fire, and honor Her both as your hidden birthplace and as the Mother of divine courage.", relatedVerseIds: ["ls-121"] },
  { id: "ls-nama-607", title: "Deveśī", category: "Hṛdaya-rājñī", summary: "She who is the protector of the gods. Īśvarī of the devas means She is supreme even over shining gods and goddesses, not merely one divinity among many but the source from which every divinity flows. Doctrinally this reverses the usual hierarchy of refuge: the gods themselves, with all their powers over rain, wealth, and war, look upward to Her, so no lesser power can finally shelter or threaten the devotee She protects. Her guardianship is therefore absolute rather than departmental. In practice, when tempted to bargain with intermediate powers for health, fortune, or success, lift the prayer one step higher to their Mistress, and rest in the protection that already protects your protectors.", relatedVerseIds: ["ls-122"] },
  { id: "ls-nama-608", title: "Daṇḍanītisthā", category: "Hṛdaya-rājñī", summary: "She who maintains the rules of jusstice without the slightest error. Daṇḍa-nīti is the rod of justice that restrains those sliding toward evil and steers them back to goodness, punishing only the ignorance that refuses the virtuous path. The commentary hears Kṛṣṇa's words that He is the rod of punishment, and reads the rod as the law of karma through which the universe is administered, the same emblem judges and assemblies carry as sustainers of justice. Hence Her severity is corrective love: the virtuous need never fear Her, while the erring are disciplined precisely so they may return. In practice, submit your own conduct to Her rod daily, correct one fault promptly, and trust karma's justice instead of resenting it.", relatedVerseIds: ["ls-122"] },
  { id: "ls-nama-609", title: "Daharākāśarūpiṇī", category: "Hṛdaya-rājñī", summary: "She who is the subtle self in the heart. Dahara-ākāśa is the small space within the heart's lotus where, say Kaṭha, Chāndogya, and Brahma-sūtra, the thumb-sized Brahman dwells in the cave of the intellect, a space that is infinite rather than a measurable place. The commentary gathers these texts to show that the body's city conceals a shrine, and the shrine an expanse, and that expanse is Her subtle form, restating Her identity with Brahman in the most intimate possible location. She is not far but inmost. In practice, withdraw attention from outer vastness, enter the imagined lotus of the heart, and search earnestly within its small sky until the seeker recognizes the sought as Herself.", relatedVerseIds: ["ls-122"] },
  { id: "ls-nama-610", title: "Pratipanmukhyarākāntatithimaṇḍalapūjitā", category: "Hṛdaya-rājñī", summary: "She who is worshipped daily starting with pratipad (first day of the lunar half-month) and ending with the full moon. Pratipad opens the bright fortnight and rākā crowns it with fullness, and the tithi-maṇḍala is the circle of fifteen lunar nityā goddesses surrounding the bindu in Śrīcakra, where Lalitāmbikā reigns as mahā-nitya amid five guardians on each side of the innermost triangle. The Vedas know these fifteen plus one more, Sadā, the perpetual sixteenth digit, while the moon's sixteen kalās from Amṛtā to Pūrṇāmṛtā wear the sixteen vowels as their names. In practice, mark each lunar day with a small offering or recitation, letting the waxing moon teach how fragmented attention gathers into fullness at Her feet.", relatedVerseIds: ["ls-122"] },
  { id: "ls-nama-611", title: "Kalātmikā", category: "Hṛdaya-rājñī", summary: "She who is in the form of the kalās. Kalā means a digit or minute fraction of a thing: the moon counts sixteen, the sun twelve, and fire ten, and the waning fortnight sheds one lunar digit daily until the void of new-moon night, then rebuilds toward fullness. The commentary crowns this arithmetic with a paradox, naming Lalitāmbikā Herself the sixteenth digit, present in the moon without ever waxing or waning, proof of a Brahman beyond modification. Sun, moon, and fire shine only by reflecting Her, and the four states of waking, dream, deep sleep, and turya each hold four digits, with the waking stage itself declared Her form. In practice, look for Her not only in grand wholes but in the subtlest fraction of anything, since nothing is too small to be Her.", relatedVerseIds: ["ls-123"] },
  { id: "ls-nama-612", title: "Kalānāthā", category: "Hṛdaya-rājñī", summary: "She who is the mistress of all the kalās. If the previous name says She is the digits, this one says She rules them, administering every subtle ingredient that composes a gross thing. The commentary notes that the moon's disc itself is likened to Śrīcakra, and since She dwells enthroned in Śrīcakra as its queen, the title Kalānāthā, Lady of the lunar digits, belongs to Her by residence as well as by nature. Nothing partial escapes Her government: phases, fractions, and fragments all answer to Her. In practice, when life feels piecemeal and incomplete, remember that the Mistress of parts already holds the whole pattern, and surrender the fragments to the One who numbers and orders them.", relatedVerseIds: ["ls-123"] },
  { id: "ls-nama-613", title: "Kāvyālāpavinodinī", category: "Hṛdaya-rājñī", summary: "She who delights in hearing poetry. Kāvya here means the great epics, measured by eighteen excellences from serenity and sincerity to magnanimity and purity, with Vālmīki's Rāmāyaṇa upheld as the first and fullest embodiment. The commentary delights in the consequence: sincere worshippers receive poetic power itself, as Kālidāsa did and as the once-dumb Mūka poet did, while Saundarya Laharī promises eloquence to whoever contemplates Her with the eight Vāc Devis who authored this hymn. A later name will even say She is those epics in person. In practice, read or recite noble verse aloud as an offering, and ask the Lover of poetry to tune your own speech toward truth and beauty.", relatedVerseIds: ["ls-123"] },
  { id: "ls-nama-614", title: "Sacāmararamāvāṇīsavyadakṣiṇasevitā", category: "Hṛdaya-rājñī", summary: "She who is attended by lakṣmī on the left side and sarasvatī on the right side, bearing ceremonial fans. Ramā fans Her on the left and Vāṇī on the right, so that wealth and learning stand as Her handmaidens rather than rival goddesses. The commentary draws the gracious corollary expounded in Saundarya Laharī and Lalitā Triśatī: those who contemplate the Queen receive prosperity and wisdom without even asking, since servants naturally follow their Mistress into the devotee's home. Power, riches, and eloquence are thus side-effects of vision, never the price of it. In practice, contemplate Her enthroned between the two attendants, and let both sufficiency and clarity arrive unsought instead of chasing either separately.", relatedVerseIds: ["ls-123"] },
  { id: "ls-nama-615", title: "Ādiśaktiḥ", category: "Hṛdaya-rājñī", summary: "She who is the primordial power, the parāśakti who is the cause of the universe. Ādi-śakti means the first energy: Śiva has no creator, and His sole creation is Śaktī, who in turn creates all worlds as His creative pulsation, the throb by which the bound soul rediscovers its divine nature. The commentary maps the seeker's ascent onto three postures of the Goddess questioning Śiva in the tantras, first seated beside Him in duality, then on His lap in approaching intimacy, then merged as Ardhanārīśvara and finally the Liṅga, the seeker likewise dissolving distance until consciousness declares I am That. In practice, notice where you sit today, beside, upon, or within, and let each prayer move you one posture closer to union.", relatedVerseIds: ["ls-124"] },
  { id: "ls-nama-616", title: "Ameyā", category: "Hṛdaya-rājñī", summary: "She who is not measurable by any means. Mortals can be measured because they are bounded, while the Eternal cannot be spanned by any instrument of mind or sense. The commentary seals this with Kaṭha Upaniṣad's celebrated paradox that the Self is smaller than the smallest and greater than the greatest, a formula repeated across the Upaniṣads precisely because measurement fails in both directions. To call Her immeasurable is therefore to confess Her Brahman-nature, since only the Absolute exceeds every scale. In practice, stop trying to size Her up with concepts or confine Her to experiences, and instead let yourself be measured by Her, becoming small enough in ego to be enfolded by the Measureless.", relatedVerseIds: ["ls-124"] },
  { id: "ls-nama-617", title: "Ātmā", category: "Hṛdaya-rājñī", summary: "She who is the self in all. The name plays on ātma the individual soul, also called jīva and puruṣa, and Ātma the universal Self or Brahman, declaring that She is both ends of the quest and its middle. The commentary explains that the soul, veiled by a body and named master of its form, worships its own Source through meditation, gradually modifying the mind until the small self recognizes the great Self that was present since primeval times, their blissful merger at the crown being the famed Śiva-Śaktī union. Ignorance never removed Her, only hid Her. In practice, sit as the small self turning its face inward, and persist in meditation until the meditator, the process, and the Self are tasted as one.", relatedVerseIds: ["ls-124"] },
  { id: "ls-nama-618", title: "Paramā", category: "Hṛdaya-rājñī", summary: "She who is the supreme. Paired with the previous name, this declares Her the universal Self itself, proving She is simultaneously saguṇa Brahman with attributes and nirguṇa Brahman beyond them. The commentary delights in the syllables para, asya, and ma, reading I am this Universal Soul, the very formula of Self-realization, while a second parsing makes Her the consort of the Beyond, Śiva's wife who personally guides seekers to Him. Liṅga Purāṇa's cascading negations, neither inward nor outward awareness, neither known nor to be known, yet immortal and most excellent, are all said to describe Her form. In practice, affirm quietly that the Supreme is your own Self, and ask the Supreme Mother to deliver you to Her Lord.", relatedVerseIds: ["ls-124"] },
  { id: "ls-nama-619", title: "Pāvanākṛtiḥ", category: "Hṛdaya-rājñī", summary: "She who is of sacred form. Pāvana means purifying, so the name says Her very shape cleanses, and Her acts of creating, sustaining, and liberating are all auspicious workings of that purity. The commentary insists She alone can lead the soul to Śiva, since realization demands the highest intellect refined by penance, knowledge, and above all Her grace, adding the startling claim that merely thinking of Her purifies, and that ceaseless remembrance outranks every ritual and meditation, which are only tools toward that unbroken cognition. Purity here is contact, not scrubbing. In practice, replace scattered rituals with one continuous thread of recollection through the day, returning to Her the instant you notice you have wandered.", relatedVerseIds: ["ls-124"] },
  { id: "ls-nama-620", title: "Anekakoṭibrahmāṇḍajananī", category: "Hṛdaya-rājñī", summary: "She who is the creator of many crores of worlds. The literal claim is staggering, Mother of billions of worlds with their galaxies, grounded in Her triple office of creating, sustaining, and dissolving. The commentary sets this vastness within a graded cosmology: as the soul passes through waking, dream, deep sleep, and turya, so Brahman unfolds as unmanifest equilibrium, then Īśvara the all-knowing cause wrapped in māyā and wearing the causal body, then the golden-womb bond holding creation together, then the cosmic person. She mothers the entire ladder. In practice, let your small biography rest inside Her enormous one, and climb inward from scattered waking through dream and silence toward the turya where multiplicity is born.", relatedVerseIds: ["ls-124"] },
  { id: "ls-nama-621", title: "Divyavigrahā", category: "Hṛdaya-rājñī", summary: "She who has a divine body. Vigraha means form, and divya lifts it beyond mortal loveliness into a beauty possible only for the Divine, the visible splendor of the Absolute condescending to shape. The commentary offers a second reading that turns beauty into battle: divya as the luminous world above and vigrahā as war, recalling Devī Māhātmya where She rose into the sky without support to confront the demon Śumbha. She is thus adorable and formidable in one body. In practice, let Her beauty draw your eyes and Her courage steady your spine, approaching the same form in tenderness when you love and in valor when you must fight inner demons.", relatedVerseIds: ["ls-124"] },
  { id: "ls-nama-622", title: "Klīṅkārī", category: "Hṛdaya-rājñī", summary: "She who is creator of the syllable 'klīṃ'. Klīṁ is the kāma-bīja, the seed of the love-god Manmatha, which Śiva Himself is said to have expounded to Śaktī out of love: ka for Kāmadeva, la for Indra, ī for contentment, and the bindu dispensing pleasure or pain according to karma. The same seed propitiates Lakṣmī, and a deeper parsing reads ka as Śiva, la as Śaktī, and īṁ as kāmakalā, so that the tiny syllable encodes the union of the divine Couple. She both authors the seed and is its presiding wife. In practice, receive klīṁ not as a charm for attraction but as condensed wedlock, and let each repetition marry awareness to its power.", relatedVerseIds: ["ls-125"] },
  { id: "ls-nama-623", title: "Kevalā", category: "Hṛdaya-rājñī", summary: "She who is the absolute, as She is complete, independent and without any attributes. Kevala means utter aloneness, the nirguṇa Brahman devoid of qualities and modifications, which Śiva-sūtra describes as sheer consciousness established in itself beyond pleasure and pain. The commentary links this austerity to the previous seed: strip ka and la from klīṁ and the remainder īṁ, the kāmakalā, stands alone as the liberating core, reservations about desire transmuted into the power that carries the soul through righteousness, wealth, and love toward final release. Both the love-seed and the love-digit are declared auspicious. In practice, peel away one attribute you cling to, and rest briefly as sheer witnessing awareness, tasting the Absolute that needs nothing added.", relatedVerseIds: ["ls-125"] },
  { id: "ls-nama-624", title: "Guhyā", category: "Hṛdaya-rājñī", summary: "She who is to be known in secret. Guhyā means hidden in both form and nature, so the tradition guards Her worship from the casual gaze: advanced rites like navāvaraṇa are closed to the uninitiated partly because authentic practice can release potent capacities that demand maturity. Yet the commentary immediately balances secrecy with transcendence, noting that realization itself lies beyond all rites, and grounding Her hiddenness in the heart-cave taught by Kaṭha and Taittirīya texts, where the thumb-sized Brahman glows like self-lit smoke within the lotus of the chest, present as breath and elements in every being. In practice, keep your deepest disciplines quietly veiled, and descend daily into the heart's cave where the Secret One already waits.", relatedVerseIds: ["ls-125"] },
  { id: "ls-nama-625", title: "Kaivalyapadadāyinī", category: "Hṛdaya-rājñī", summary: "She who bestows liberation. Kaivalya is the soul's final aloneness, the hour when, helpers fallen away, it must achieve its own passage: either it slides back toward rebirth through desire, or it rises through samādhi into union never to return. Pada here unfolds as four ascending intimacies, dwelling in the same world as God, wearing His form, standing near Him, and finally merging with Him, with kaivalya shimmering beyond even merger, each stage attained according to ripeness. Lalitāmbikā is the giver of this summit, escorting the solitary soul across it. In practice, locate yourself honestly among the four steps, and beg Her to graduate you from worshipping images to wearing likeness, nearness, merger, and beyond.", relatedVerseIds: ["ls-125"] },
  { id: "ls-nama-626", title: "Tripurā", category: "Hṛdaya-rājñī", summary: "She who is older than the three (trinity of brahmā viṣṇu and shiva). Tri-purā is She who precedes and pervades every triad, the subject of the Tripura-rahasya dialogue between Bhārgava and Dattātreya on Her glory, Her rites, and Her knowledge. The commentary parades the threes She wears: creator, preserver, and dissolver; will, wisdom, and action; the three mantra-peaks; birth, maintenance, and death; the three breath-channels; the three worlds; the three guṇas; even the three veils over consciousness through intellect, knowledge, and individuality. Older means She survives their dissolution. In practice, take any trio governing your life, body, mind, and circumstance, and step behind it toward the One who was already there before the count began.", relatedVerseIds: ["ls-125"] },
  { id: "ls-nama-627", title: "Trijagadvandyā", category: "Hṛdaya-rājñī", summary: "She who is adored by the inhabitants of all three worlds. The three worlds of bhūr, bhuvar, and suvar, carried in the three calls of the Gāyatrī, receive worship from every level of existence because every level depends on Her. Doctrinally these worlds double as three grades of awareness, from dense material consciousness through intermediate brightness to the purest knowing that is Brahman itself, so that adoration ascends as consciousness clarifies. None adore outside Her jurisdiction and none need to. In practice, offer Her worship from exactly the level you occupy today, gross need, mixed motive, or pure love, trusting that inhabitants of all three worlds includes you, and that She accepts the salute of each.", relatedVerseIds: ["ls-125"] },
  { id: "ls-nama-628", title: "Trimūrtiḥ", category: "Hṛdaya-rājñī", summary: "She who is the aggregate of the trinity (brahmā, viṣṇu and shiva). She is the combined form of Brahmā, Viṣṇu, and Rudra, born, says the retold story, when the three gods exchanged glances and a maiden appeared who named Herself their united Śaktī, robed in white, black, and red for the three strands of nature. The commentary presses the metaphysics further: Brahman divides into the three for cosmic labor, and the three reunite as Śaktī, One becoming many and many returning to One, cause flowering as effect without ever ceasing to be cause. She is therefore not a fourth alongside them but their total. In practice, stop compartmentalizing creation, preservation, and endings in your life, and meet each as the same Mother changing costume.", relatedVerseIds: ["ls-125"] },
  { id: "ls-nama-629", title: "Tridaśeśvarī", category: "Hṛdaya-rājñī", summary: "She who is the ruler of the gods. Tri-daśas are the ever-youthful ones who never pass through childhood, age, and decay as humans do, and She is their Īśvarī and chief. The commentary counts the classic thirty-three, twelve suns, eight Vasus, eleven Rudras, and the twin physicians, each commanding ten million attendants to make the proverbial thirty-three crores, all filed under Her sovereignty, and adds that She equally rules the three nightly passages of sleep, dream, and deep rest. Divinity in heaven and vulnerability in slumber share one Mistress. In practice, commend both your waking helpers and your sleeping hours to Her, asking the Commander of thirty-three crores to guard the one small life she has assigned to you.", relatedVerseIds: ["ls-125"] },
  { id: "ls-nama-630", title: "Tryakṣari", category: "Hṛdaya-rājñī", summary: "She whose form consists of three letters or syllables (Om = a u m). She is compacted into threes: the Bālā mantra's three seeds, and the three peaks of the fifteen-syllabled mantra counted as three seeds in the sixteen-syllabled rite. The commentary summons Bṛhadāraṇyaka's riddles that hṛdaya the heart and satya truth are each three syllables, so that heart and truth already spell Brahman, and every sacred triple becomes a footpath to Her, crowned by the definition of Brahman as being, awareness, and infinity. Three is Her shorthand. In practice, sound Om slowly as three melts into one, feel heart and truth converge in the chest, and let the triad deliver you past counting into the Countless.", relatedVerseIds: ["ls-126"] },
  { id: "ls-nama-631", title: "Divyagandhāḍhyā", category: "Hṛdaya-rājñī", summary: "She who is richly endowed with divine fragrance. Divya-gandha is unearthly scent, traced to three springs: Her own form which is fragrance itself, the naturally sweet attendants thronging Her, and the sandal and perfumes devotees offer, all diffusing together across the worlds. The commentary adds a yogic seal drawn from Patañjali on transcending the senses: when meditation ripens past its cogitated stage, the meditator's very surroundings turn fragrant, smell outgrowing its organ as mind and consciousness overshoot perception's limits. Fragrance thus becomes both offering and evidence. In practice, offer Her real scent with devotion, and if sweetness ever visits your own prayer unsummoned, receive it as a milestone and pass beyond it toward the Scentless.", relatedVerseIds: ["ls-126"] },
  { id: "ls-nama-632", title: "Sindūratilakāñcitā", category: "Hṛdaya-rājñī", summary: "She who shines witha vermillion mark on Her forehead; She who is decorated with a special paste made of vermilion. The red tilaka on Her brow is the condensed sign of auspiciousness, marriage, and power, proclaiming that all good fortune sits visibly upon Her face. The commentary adds a second, stranger reading in which the same syllables picture Her ringed by she-elephants, massive grace attending grace, strength made gentle around the Queen. Whether read as cosmetic or cosmic, the name dresses Her for blessing. In practice, gaze at that red mark until your own forehead seems to warm with it, and carry one deliberate act of auspiciousness into the day as Her tilaka upon your conduct.", relatedVerseIds: ["ls-126"] },
  { id: "ls-nama-633", title: "Umā", category: "Hṛdaya-rājñī", summary: "She who is pārvatī devī. Umā is the mountain-king's daughter who began austerities at five to win Maheśvara, and the name overflows with doctrine: Śiva-sūtra hails Umā as the splendor of Śiva, will-power in constant communion with Him and His ever-free I-consciousness in knowing and doing, while her syllables U, M, and A encode creation, dissolution, and sustenance of the praṇava. Liṅga Purāṇa crowns Her mother of worlds, Rudra-born rebuker of Dakṣa reborn to captivate Rudra's mind toward Skanda's birth, and Kena Upaniṣad salutes Her as the radiant Haimavatī. Even the heart's unstruck resonance and the Śaktī-seed are called Umā. In practice, convert one act of will today into communion, choosing what Śiva would choose until your I tastes Hers.", relatedVerseIds: ["ls-126"] },
  { id: "ls-nama-634", title: "Śailendratanayā", category: "Hṛdaya-rājñī", summary: "She who is the daughter of himavat, the king of the mountains. Himavān is immovability itself, and his daughter is pure caitanya, consciousness distilled from pure knowledge, the celebrated fourth or turya condition beyond waking, dream, and sleep. The commentary draws the quiet corollary: if the Absolute is born of the Mountain, then Brahman dwells even in the immovable, and stone is not excluded from spirit. Daughterhood here means manifestation, the Unmoving appearing as the All-knowing. In practice, lend your body the mountain's stillness for a few minutes, spine like rock and breath like wind around it, and notice that awareness does not freeze with the frame but shines all the clearer within it.", relatedVerseIds: ["ls-126"] },
  { id: "ls-nama-635", title: "Gaurī", category: "Hṛdaya-rājñī", summary: "She who has a fair complexion. Gaura is radiant golden fairness, white melting into yellow like conch, jasmine, and moon, the three similes tradition chooses not only for color but because each is supremely auspicious. The commentary notes the name's wider echoes, borne also by Varuṇa's consort and by the ten-year-old maiden in the age-graded goddess, locating Gaurī at the threshold where childhood ripens toward power. Fairness here is luminosity made flesh, purity you can see. In practice, picture Her moon-jasmine fairness at dawn, and let one thought, one sentence, and one deed today carry that same untarnished whiteness into a stained world.", relatedVerseIds: ["ls-126"] },
  { id: "ls-nama-636", title: "Gandharvasevitā", category: "Hṛdaya-rājñī", summary: "She who is served by gandharvas (like viśvāvasu). Gandharvas are the sky-dwelling singers of the air and heavenly waters, twelve principals led by names like Tumburu, Nārada, Viśvāvasu, and Citrasena, two presiding over each month and hymning even the sun with their music. Yet the commentary stresses their limits: despite celestial song they remain subject to misery, so they too worship Her, with Hāhā and Hūhū singled out in Lalitā Triśatī as Her choristers. Art itself kneels here. In practice, let whatever music you love become service rather than escape, offering one song upward today, and remember that even the sweetest voices find their pitch from Her silence.", relatedVerseIds: ["ls-126"] },
  { id: "ls-nama-637", title: "Viśvagarbhā", category: "Hṛdaya-rājñī", summary: "She who contains the whole universe in Her womb. Viśva is everything visible, the entire spread cosmos, and garbha declares it unborn-yet-held, carried within Her like a child not yet delivered. The commentary links this forward to the later salutation of Viśvamātā, since the Mother of the universe must first be its bearer: delivery presupposes pregnancy, and creation is Her labor. Space, time, and beings are therefore interior to Her before they are exterior to us. In practice, reverse your sense of containment, instead of holding a small image of Her inside your mind, rest yourself as a small stirring inside Her vast womb, and let Her breathing move your world.", relatedVerseIds: ["ls-127"] },
  { id: "ls-nama-638", title: "Svarṇagarbhā", category: "Hṛdaya-rājñī", summary: "She who is the cause of the universe. Svarṇa-garbhā, the golden womb, names the first-born cosmic soul distinct from the later trinity, whose superior subtle body of vital forces, mind, intellect, and senses is the cosmic intellect itself, while our lesser subtle bodies form the ego through which karma is tasted. So She mothers both the world's mind and our minds. The commentary adds a luminous pun: splendid letters shining within a womb, meaning She also gestates the sacred alphabets, the mātṛkā powers behind every mantra already praised as Her letter-form. Mantra and cosmos are twin pregnancies. In practice, honor each breath and each syllable of japa as movement inside Her golden womb, and recite the alphabet slowly as Her shining jewelry.", relatedVerseIds: ["ls-127"] },
  { id: "ls-nama-639", title: "Avaradā", category: "Hṛdaya-rājñī", summary: "She who destroys the unholy. Avara-dā slays the demons, but the commentary widens the battlefield inward: the unholy means not only doers of evil acts but harborers of evil thoughts, since thoughts left undisturbed ripen unfailingly into deeds. Her destruction is therefore preventive as well as punitive, striking corruption at its seed-stage before it can arm itself as action. No deformity of the heart escapes Her jurisdiction merely by remaining unacted. In practice, invite Her inspection early, naming one unworthy thought the moment it surfaces, and let Her destroy it in the cradle rather than summoning the strength to fight it full-grown on the field.", relatedVerseIds: ["ls-127"] },
  { id: "ls-nama-640", title: "Vāgadhīśvarī", category: "Hṛdaya-rājñī", summary: "She who presides over speech. Adhi-īśvarī of vāk means every word ever spoken moves within Her governorship, from cosmic sound down to gossip. The commentary recalls Saundarya Laharī closing with the confession that its hundred verses were composed with Her own words lent to the poet, and points back to the letter-powers and the staged origin of sound expounded across earlier names: speech is Her circuitry, not man's invention. To speak is to borrow. In practice, dedicate the day's speech to its Ruler each morning, pausing before consequential sentences to ask whether She would sign them, and return one prayer aloud as rent for the borrowed tongue.", relatedVerseIds: ["ls-127"] },
  { id: "ls-nama-641", title: "Dhyānagamyā", category: "Hṛdaya-rājñī", summary: "She who is to be attained through meditation. Gamya means reachable, and dhyāna names the road: not daydreaming about splendid forms but disciplined inner research that explores the whole human potential, a progressive quickening of consciousness driven by intention wedded to attention. The commentary cites Śvetāśvatara's sages who entered deep meditation and saw the luminous Cosmic Self behind the universe, veiled by māyā yet controlling every self and even time, and declares that Self to be Her. Meditation therefore arrives because its object was already the ground. In practice, meditate as a researcher rather than a fantasist, holding one intention steadily and watching attention deepen until the Cosmic Self looks back through your own gaze.", relatedVerseIds: ["ls-127"] },
  { id: "ls-nama-642", title: "Aparicchedyā", category: "Hṛdaya-rājñī", summary: "She whose limits cannot be ascertained (unlimited). Pariccheda is boundary-drawing, and its negation says no line can be drawn around Her: incomprehensible, therefore infinite, therefore ubiquitous, the signature quality of Brahman. The commentary pairs this vastness with the previous name's tenderness, holding the paradox intact, that the Boundless who defeats every measure still lets devotees reach Her through meditation. Unknowability by concept does not mean unavailability to love. In practice, alternate the two movements daily, confess with the mind that She exceeds every frame you make, then sit with the heart as though distance never existed, letting infinity and intimacy correct each other.", relatedVerseIds: ["ls-127"] },
  { id: "ls-nama-643", title: "Jñānadā", category: "Hṛdaya-rājñī", summary: "She who gives knowledge of the self. The knowledge She gives is not information but the pure, sense-untainted knowing by which Her absolute form is realized, since Brahman yields only to unvitiated cognition. The commentary describes a triple grace: first She supplies the requisite understanding, then She unveils Herself to it, and finally She dissolves the knower into Herself, turning mundane learning into consummate wisdom through meditation. Śiva-sūtra's warning that limited knowledge is bondage becomes Her promise inverted, that unlimited knowledge is release. In practice, pray for knowing before asking for favors, study one liberating sentence until it alters perception, and offer every insight back as thanks to its Giver.", relatedVerseIds: ["ls-127"] },
  { id: "ls-nama-644", title: "Jñānavigrahā", category: "Hṛdaya-rājñī", summary: "She who is sthe embodiment of knowledge itself. If the previous name says She gives knowledge, this one names its reservoir: Her entire body is the substance given, every limb a doctrine and every ornament a thesis. The commentary illustrates embodied transmission through Ramaṇa Maharṣi, who initiated without mantras by a single transforming glance, and through the modern insight that consciousness makes reality appear within itself rather than merely registering it. Knowledge here is contagious presence before it is proposition. In practice, place yourself before Her image as before a living text, gaze quietly rather than chattering requests, and let darshan do the teaching that sentences cannot carry.", relatedVerseIds: ["ls-127"] },
  { id: "ls-nama-645", title: "Sarvavedāntasaṃvedyā", category: "Hṛdaya-rājñī", summary: "She who is known by all of vedānta. Vedānta, the crown of knowledge, gathers the Vedas' four ascending layers of hymn, ritual, forest-reflection, and Upaniṣadic summit, condensed by Vyāsa into the Brahma-sūtras, and crowned by Kṛṣṇa's claim to be the sole object worth knowing through them. The commentary insists that only this subtlest tier discloses Her true form, since gross rites and bright hymns prepare the climber but the summit-view belongs to the Upaniṣads' refined inquiry. All Vedānta converges on one face. In practice, treat Upaniṣadic study as portrait-gazing rather than homework, reading slowly until a sentence about Brahman suddenly reads as a sentence about Her, and then stop reading and contemplate.", relatedVerseIds: ["ls-128"] },
  { id: "ls-nama-646", title: "Satyānandasvarūpiṇī", category: "Hṛdaya-rājñī", summary: "She whose form is existence and bliss. Satya is truth and ānanda undying happiness, and the name binds them as one nature: lasting joy cannot be reached by bypassing reality. The commentary glosses truth as non-dual wisdom, citing Taittirīya's vision of Brahman manifest as all things, which knowers simply call the True, so that recognizing the One everywhere flowers inevitably into delight. Eternity is included, since what is truly real never expires, and spiritual climbers reportedly taste the bliss before they fully see its ground. In practice, refuse one comfortable falsehood today, great or petty, and watch how honesty lightens the chest, letting that small ānanda tutor you in Her greater one.", relatedVerseIds: ["ls-128"] },
  { id: "ls-nama-647", title: "Lopāmudrārcitā", category: "Hṛdaya-rājñī", summary: "She who is worshipped by lopāmudrā (wife of sage agastya). Lopāmudrā's devotion was so renowned that, says the Triśatī's introductory story, when Hayagrīva hesitated to transmit the three hundred names, Lalitāmbikā Herself appeared with Śiva and commanded the initiation, praising Agastya's wife who adores Her with the fifteen-syllabled mantra. Their joint composition is extolled as the all-completing hymn that fulfills unfinished karmas and ripens liberation. A mortal woman's worship thus moved the cosmic Couple to open a sealed scripture. In practice, emulate Lopāmudrā's loyal secrecy and regularity rather than chasing novelty, reciting an allotted portion daily, and trust quiet fidelity to accomplish what brilliance cannot.", relatedVerseIds: ["ls-128"] },
  { id: "ls-nama-648", title: "LīlākLLiptabrahmāṇḍamaṇḍalā", category: "Hṛdaya-rājñī", summary: "She who has created and maintained the universe purely as a sport. Līlā means play: She frames the cosmos the way a master frames a game, authoring the three acts of emergence, maintenance, and dissolution and their single great rule, the law of karma, which She Herself never transgresses. The commentary finds Her ease explained here, since perfect obedience to self-made law makes omnipotent labor feel light, the spheres administered without strain. Effortlessness is not indifference but mastery. In practice, stop picturing providence as anxious toil, trust the karmic rules as the fixed lines of a fair game, and play your own difficult part with something of Her sporting grace.", relatedVerseIds: ["ls-128"] },
  { id: "ls-nama-649", title: "Adṛśyā", category: "Hṛdaya-rājñī", summary: "She who is not perceived by sense organs (normal eyes). Adṛśyā declares Her invisible, not absent but too subtle for biology, being woven of kāmakalā and kuṇḍalinī currents that outrun every instrument. The commentary summons Bṛhadāraṇyaka's riddle that the witness of vision cannot itself be seen, since sight reveals objects while the seeing Self pervades unseen, and concludes that She is realized only in consciousness through meditation. Eyes report Her effects; awareness meets Her. In practice, cease hunting for visions and turn attention upon attention itself, resting as the witness behind the eyes until the Invisible becomes more certain than anything the eyes ever showed.", relatedVerseIds: ["ls-129"] },
  { id: "ls-nama-650", title: "Dṛśyarahitā", category: "Hṛdaya-rājñī", summary: "She who has nothing to see. The negation turns around: not only can senses not see Her, there is nothing outside Her for even Her to behold. The commentary reads this as the sequel to invisibility, assigning the previous name to Her kāmakalā radiance and this one to Her kuṇḍalinī depth, both resolving into supreme knowledge beyond comprehension. With no second thing existing, seer and spectacle are one, and objectless awareness is Her mode. In practice, borrow Her posture in meditation, withdraw interest from every arising image including sacred ones, and abide as contentless knowing, discovering that when there is nothing left to see, the Seer finally stands revealed.", relatedVerseIds: ["ls-129"] },
  { id: "ls-nama-651", title: "Vijñātrī", category: "Hṛdaya-rājñī", summary: "She who knows the truth of the physical universe. Vijñātṛ means the Knower, and total knowing is claimed as Brahman's exclusive signature, sealed by Bṛhadāraṇyaka's unanswerable question of through what one could know That by which all is known, the Knower Himself. The commentary draws the contemplative consequence: the realized person discriminates the Real from the unreal until only the subject remains, Absolute and without a second, and that remainder is Her. She is not one knower among many but Knowing itself. In practice, practice discrimination once a day, setting aside one unreal anxiety, and let the Knower in you recognize Herself, resting as the subject that no object can capture.", relatedVerseIds: ["ls-129"] },
  { id: "ls-nama-652", title: "Vedyavarjitā", category: "Hṛdaya-rājñī", summary: "She who has nothing left to know. Vedya is the knowable, and its absence says omniscience so complete that no object remains outstanding, the fitting seal upon the previous name's Knower. The commentary treats this brevity as affirmation rather than addition: having declared Her all-knowing, it simply closes the circle, since a Brahman with homework still to do would not be Brahman. Finished knowledge is Her repose. In practice, imitate that repose in miniature by ending one curiosity a day, closing an open mental tab of gossip or speculation, and resting briefly in the sufficiency of what you already truly know, letting Her completeness tutor your restlessness.", relatedVerseIds: ["ls-129"] },
  { id: "ls-nama-653", title: "Yoginī", category: "Hṛdaya-rājñī", summary: "She who is constantly united with parāśiva; She who possesses the power of yoga. Yoga means the yoking of self to Self, and She stands eternally so yoked, harmonizing body, mind, and soul toward the soul's emancipation from bondage. The commentary widens the lens to Her retinue: yoginīs preside over Śrīcakra's nine enclosures and, as Ḍākinī and her sisters, govern the spinal centers already mapped in earlier names, while astrologers read yoginī as a time-quality coloring human hours. She is both the Union and the host of its guardians. In practice, harmonize one discord today, breath with posture or intention with act, and salute the inner guardians by tending the center you inhabit.", relatedVerseIds: ["ls-129"] },
  { id: "ls-nama-654", title: "Yogadā", category: "Hṛdaya-rājñī", summary: "She who bestows the power of yoga. Dā means gift, and the gift here is union itself: the soul's reunion with God is not seized but received. The commentary compresses the whole grace-doctrine into one line, teaching that even the desire for yoga dawns only by Her favor, so that longing is already Her first installment. No one climbs uninvited. In practice, if practice feels dry, stop striving for technique and ask for the appetite itself, praying to want God more truly, and count every stirring of aspiration, however faint, as Her hand already extended toward yours. Then cooperate with the gift by sitting down to practice today.", relatedVerseIds: ["ls-129"] },
  { id: "ls-nama-655", title: "Yogyā", category: "Hṛdaya-rājñī", summary: "She who deserves yoga of all kinds. Yogya means She is the worthy object of every discipline, completing a triad with the two prior names: She is yoga itself, its giver, and its goal, enjoyed, enjoyed-through, and enjoyer all at once in Brahman. The commentary gathers witnesses, Śvetāśvatara commanding knowledge of the indwelling Brahman beyond which nothing higher stands, Kṛṣṇa defining yoga as the sorrowless state to be pursued with unwearied resolve, and Patañjali defining it as the stilling of mind's restless turnings. Every road of union ends at Her door because every road is Hers. In practice, choose one discipline and pursue it resolutely without shopping for methods, trusting the worthy Goal to honor steady feet.", relatedVerseIds: ["ls-129"] },
  { id: "ls-nama-656", title: "Yogānandā", category: "Hṛdaya-rājñī", summary: "She who is the bliss attained through yoga; She who enjoys the bliss of yoga. She tastes bliss in attaining Śiva, and devotees taste it in attaining Her, yet until bliss ripens two still stand facing each other, and past it only One remains without a second. The commentary offers deep sleep as nature's sample, the mind switched off while the body hums on, a nightly foretaste of unattached delight, and parses the syllables as freedom-from-clinging wedded to joy, the imperishable Unattached of the Upaniṣad joined to the river, the mountain stream, the glad lunar days, and even the cowherd's divine Child. In practice, rehearse conscious sleep, lying still while staying aware, and learn to be happy with nothing happening.", relatedVerseIds: ["ls-129"] },
  { id: "ls-nama-657", title: "Yugandharā", category: "Hṛdaya-rājñī", summary: "She who is the bearer of the yugas. Yuga means an age, and the cosmos breathes through four, golden, silver, bronze, and the present iron Kali counted from 3102 before the common era, their full wheel of over four million years ending in great dissolution before creation dawns again, all carried and steered by Her. The commentary adds the yoke-sense of the same word: She is the beam joining the paired oxen, coupling Śiva to Herself so intimately that neither functions independently. Time's bearer is also Love's harness. In practice, entrust both your private season and this public iron age to Her shoulders, and consent daily to be yoked beside Śiva in whatever furrow the hour ploughs.", relatedVerseIds: ["ls-129"] },
  { id: "ls-nama-658", title: "Icchāśaktijñānaśaktikriyāśaktisvarūpiṇī", category: "Hṛdaya-rājñī", summary: "She who is in the form of the powers of will, knowledge and action. The triple energies unfold creation in order: consciousness poised to expand is will, its actual self-unfolding is wisdom, and diversification into worlds is action, She remaining undifferentiated from Śiva while wielding all three. The commentary frames this as the analysis of the threefold, mistress of every triad from cosmic labor to mantra, crowned by Tantrāloka's vision of action-power reflecting sun, moon, and fire until the indivisible point of Light dawns as the Supreme. To possess all three energies is to be parā-śakti. In practice, audit one undertaking through Her three lenses, clarifying what you truly will, what you actually know, and what you finally do.", relatedVerseIds: ["ls-130"] },
  { id: "ls-nama-659", title: "Sarvādhārā", category: "Hṛdaya-rājñī", summary: "She who is the support of all. Ādhārā means the bearer beneath, and all things between gross earth and Her subtlest kuṇḍalinī form rest upon Her without exception. The commentary shows the mechanism inherited from the previous name: She wants to uphold, She knows how to uphold, and Her threefold doing of creating, sustaining, and dissolving performs the upholding, will, wisdom, and act converging in single motherhood. Nothing hangs unsupported. In practice, notice what you are straining to hold together today, reputation, family, or future, and deliberately transfer its weight downward onto the Support, relaxing your grip while keeping your hands faithfully at their post.", relatedVerseIds: ["ls-130"] },
  { id: "ls-nama-660", title: "Supratīṣṭhā", category: "Hṛdaya-rājñī", summary: "She who is firmly established. Pratiṣṭhā goes deeper than support: She is the foundation under the support, such that without Her willing, knowing, and acting nothing at all could exist. The commentary hears Kṛṣṇa's promise of the further Unmanifest beyond the unmanifest, the imperishable supreme Abode from which travelers never return to mortal exile, and identifies that bedrock as Her. Foundations do not tremble when walls do. In practice, test what you are building upon, opinion, wealth, or health, and sink one footing deeper today into the well-founded One, choosing a single act of fidelity that would hold even if the walls shook.", relatedVerseIds: ["ls-130"] },
  { id: "ls-nama-661", title: "Sadasadrūpadhāriṇī", category: "Hṛdaya-rājñī", summary: "She who assumes the forms of both being and non-being. Sat is the permanent and asat the impermanent: Brahman alone endures, so the whole universe of names and forms counts as non-being beside it, yet She wears both wardrobes at once. The commentary traces the riddle through Taittirīya's telling that the world lay non-existent within unmanifest Brahman before manifesting, Chāndogya's challenge of how something rises from apparent nothing, and Patañjali's warning about words without realities, concluding that the impermanent is the product of the Permanent, the universe Śaktī flowering from Brahman-Śiva. In practice, hold both truths together, treating persons and projects as real enough to love and unreal enough to release, and let every ending remind you of the Permanence wearing it.", relatedVerseIds: ["ls-130"] },
  { id: "ls-nama-662", title: "Aṣṭamūrtiḥ", category: "Hṛdaya-rājñī", summary: "She who has eight forms. Eight is Her number on every plane: Śiva's eight cosmic faces and Her own eight mother-powers from Brāhmī to Mahālakṣmī, the soul's eight grades from entangled jīva to the elemental self, and Kṛṣṇa's eightfold lower nature of earth, water, fire, air, ether, mind, reason, and ego. The commentary even counts the five elements plus sun, moon, and soul as eight bodies, and honors the eight Vāc Devis who authored this very hymn. She is the octave in which reality is scored. In practice, survey your own eightfold instrument of senses, mind, and ego with gratitude, and offer each of the eight strings to be tuned by the Musician who owns them all.", relatedVerseIds: ["ls-131"] },
  { id: "ls-nama-663", title: "Ajājetrī", category: "Hṛdaya-rājñī", summary: "She who conquers ignorance. Ajā is the unborn darkness of not-knowing, and jetrī is its female conqueror, a paradox since She is knowledge itself with no ignorance to defeat. The commentary resolves it through Śvetāśvatara's she-goat of nature in red, white, and black, toward which the ignorant rush with delight while the experienced turn away: victory means lending devotees the discriminating sight to reject the sense-world's glitter. Her conquest happens inside us, since darkness cannot approach light but must be routed where it nests. In practice, stage one small skirmish today, refusing a single alluring distraction you have already learned to regret, and dedicate the tiny triumph to the Conqueress fighting through you.", relatedVerseIds: ["ls-131"] },
  { id: "ls-nama-664", title: "Lokayātravidhāyinī", category: "Hṛdaya-rājñī", summary: "She who directs the course of the worlds. Loka-yātrā is the journey of the worlds, each body born, nourished, and dissolved, each soul disembarking from one form into another except the rare few who voyage no more. The commentary assigns Her the whole traffic of this transmigration, scheduling every departure and arrival across the three divine acts. No journey is unescorted and no timetable accidental. In practice, view your present life as one scheduled leg rather than the whole expedition, travel it responsibly with luggage light, and pray to the Directress of routes that your next embarkation find you readier, or better still, finished with voyaging.", relatedVerseIds: ["ls-131"] },
  { id: "ls-nama-665", title: "Ekākinī", category: "Hṛdaya-rājñī", summary: "She who is the lone one. Ekākinī performs all three cosmic acts entirely alone, without partner or assistant, the One without a second praised by Chāndogya before manifestation ever stirred. The commentary gathers fearlessness from solitude through Bṛhadāraṇyaka's question of what the Alone could fear, explains multiplicity as Her own reflections mistaken for others through ignorance, and crowns the point with the Vedic maxim that the wise call the One Existent by many names. Administration needs no committee when the Administrator is everything. In practice, rehearse chosen aloneness briefly each day, sitting without company or device, and discover that the solitude you feared is actually Her exclusive company.", relatedVerseIds: ["ls-131"] },
  { id: "ls-nama-666", title: "Bhūmarūpā", category: "Hṛdaya-rājñī", summary: "She who is the aggregate of all existing things. Bhūman is the Infinite, declared identical with Brahman by the sūtras, and Chāndogya's equation rings through the commentary: the infinite alone is happiness, while nothing finite satisfies. The Vāc Devis therefore re-crown Her as Brahman after cataloguing its qualities, adding Kūrma Purāṇa's image of Śiva's single energy multiplying through limiting costumes of size, shape, and quality to play every role in His presence. Vastness is Her body; limitation is Her acting. In practice, catch yourself hunting happiness in some finite portion, praise, portion, or possession, and redirect the thirst toward the Infinite, asking Bhūmā Herself to be the fullness no fragment can supply.", relatedVerseIds: ["ls-131"] },
  { id: "ls-nama-667", title: "Nirdvaitā", category: "Hṛdaya-rājñī", summary: "She who is without the sense of duality. Nir-dvaita denies even the feeling of twoness, planting Her flag in non-dualism among the three classical schools: duality that separates God, soul, and world; qualified non-dualism that binds the latter two in dependence; and advaita where all three are one substance, plurality being māyā's shimmer. The commentary confirms Her as the lonesome Brahman of advaita already announced in the solitude-name, since where none coexists with Her, counting to two is the error. In practice, interrogate one adversarial divide in your heart, self against other or sacred against profane, and soften it by looking for the single Awareness lighting both sides of the supposed fence.", relatedVerseIds: ["ls-131"] },
  { id: "ls-nama-668", title: "Dvaitavarjitā", category: "Hṛdaya-rājñī", summary: "She who is beyond duality. Where the previous name denied duality's feeling, this one bars its fact: Brahman and soul are not two, since all beings are Her reflections and duality's road never reaches realization. The commentary presents the pair as deliberate repetition, hammering the non-dual seal upon Her Brahman-status so no devotee settles for a philosophy of eternal distance. Beyond means not far away but categorically inapplicable. In practice, when prayer feels like addressing a stranger across a gap, remember that the gap itself is the illusion under review, and pray instead as a wave briefly forgetting it is ocean, letting the forgetting itself dissolve.", relatedVerseIds: ["ls-131"] },
  { id: "ls-nama-669", title: "Annadā", category: "Hṛdaya-rājñī", summary: "She who is the giver of food to all living things. Anna descends from the root to eat, and Taittirīya's physiology of grace declares that beings are born of food, sustained by food, and dissolved into it, food itself being the remedy for creaturely ills. The commentary reads this name as the charter of Her sustaining act: providence is not occasional miracle but daily bread extended universe-wide. Every harvest is Her hand. In practice, eat one meal today as conscious sacrament, acknowledging its Giver before tasting, sharing some portion with another creature as Her distributor, and letting gratitude season the food better than spice.", relatedVerseIds: ["ls-132"] },
  { id: "ls-nama-670", title: "Vasudā", category: "Hṛdaya-rājñī", summary: "She who is the giver of wealth. Vasu spans precious metals and stones, excellence and beneficence, and the eight elemental Vedic gods from waters to dawn-light under Indra's lead, so Her giving covers both bullion and blessing. The commentary cites Bṛhadāraṇyaka's great birthless Self who both eats food and grants riches to whoever truly knows Him, prescribing meditation upon that Self as the lawful mint of prosperity. Wealth rightly flows from realization rather than grasping. In practice, meditate upon the Self as your treasury before pursuing any gain, earn and spend as its trustee, and dedicate a fixed share of increase to good works as interest paid to the Giver.", relatedVerseIds: ["ls-132"] },
  { id: "ls-nama-671", title: "Vṛddhā", category: "Hṛdaya-rājñī", summary: "She who is ancient. Vṛddhā means the Old One, first because She is root-nature and primal power, born earliest of all since Śiva fashioned Her and She fashioned the rest. The commentary crowns this with Śvetāśvatara's all-form verse, woman and man, boy and girl, even the greybeard leaning on a stick, as one of the finest utterances of I am That, while insisting She Herself never ages, being beyond the modifications that wear mortals down. Primordial yet ageless is Her paradox. In practice, honor the ancient in every aged face you meet, and within your own aging frame locate the Witness that has never grown a day older, letting Her timelessness console your time-bound flesh.", relatedVerseIds: ["ls-132"] },
  { id: "ls-nama-672", title: "Brahmātmaikyasvarūpiṇī", category: "Hṛdaya-rājñī", summary: "She whose nature is the union of brahman and Atman. After surveying Brahman's qualities, the Vāc Devis unveil the summit: She is the very form of the merger between Śiva the universal and jīva the individual, the lived I am Brahman that ends all seeking. The commentary dissects the syllables into this same equation, reading Her as the haṁsa mantra breathing within every chest, ha for Śaktī riding the exhalation and sa for Śiva on the inhalation, some twenty-one thousand six hundred unions a day for whoever synchronizes awareness with breath. Liberation is respired, not merely reasoned. In practice, ride the breath consciously for a few rounds, hearing the outgoing and incoming whispers marry soul to Source until the breather dissolves.", relatedVerseIds: ["ls-132"] },
  { id: "ls-nama-673", title: "Bṛhatī", category: "Hṛdaya-rājñī", summary: "She who is immense. Bṛhatī is sheer magnitude, greater than the greatest in Kaṭha's phrase, the Absolute overflowing every container of thought. The commentary adds the prosodist's footnote that Bṛhatī is also a thirty-six-syllabled meter, as though even poetry must stretch itself to house Her, vastness wearing a metrical garment. Greatness here is not bulk but inexhaustibility. In practice, deliberately widen one narrow thing today, a cramped opinion, a grudging allowance, or a hurried minute, stretching it toward Her scale, and let the felt spaciousness become worship of the Immense who contains your expansion without effort. Greatness always has room for one more small soul.", relatedVerseIds: ["ls-132"] },
  { id: "ls-nama-674", title: "Brāhmaṇī", category: "Hṛdaya-rājñī", summary: "She who is predominantly sāttvic. Brāhmaṇī is the feminine of the Knower, Śiva's counterpart as Bhairavī is Bhairava's, marking Her the luminous, sattva-predominant face of the Absolute. The commentary confronts birth-caste confusion head-on, defining the true brahmin by Gītā's virtues of serenity, restraint, austerity, purity, forbearance, and uprightness, and by Chāndogya's prayer to attain a brahmin's fame through knowing Brahman or mastering sacred wisdom. Quality, not pedigree, is the claim. In practice, audit yourself against that ancient job description, strengthening one listed virtue this week, and honor learning and lucidity wherever they appear as Her saffron signature. Birth never bars the path; conduct alone opens or closes it.", relatedVerseIds: ["ls-132"] },
  { id: "ls-nama-675", title: "Brāhmī", category: "Hṛdaya-rājñī", summary: "She who presides over speech. Brāhmī is Sarasvatī Herself, goddess of utterance and one of the eight Mothers, stationed at the throne of language to govern every syllable. The commentary savors the grammar that Brahman turns feminine as Brāhmī, exactly as Bhairava softens into Bhairavī, implying that the Absolute's creative, expressive, mothering side has a name and a sceptre. Speech is therefore a department of state with a divine minister. In practice, bring your talking under Her administration, consecrating study, song, and conversation as temple business, and when words fail or wound, appeal upward to the Minister to repair what Her ministry mishandled.", relatedVerseIds: ["ls-132"] },
  { id: "ls-nama-676", title: "Brahmānandā", category: "Hṛdaya-rājñī", summary: "She who is ever immersed in the bliss of brahman. Brahma-ānanda is not pleasure visiting from outside but the Absolute's own happiness in which She permanently bathes. The commentary leans on Taittirīya's triple charter that beings are born from bliss, supported by bliss, and dissolve back into it, bliss being realized in the sheath of knowledge where knower and known begin to merge. Joy is thus our native element, exile the anomaly. In practice, trace any genuine gladness backward today instead of merely spending it, asking what ocean this wave rose from, and let the trail lead through gratitude into the Bliss that was already holding you.", relatedVerseIds: ["ls-132"] },
  { id: "ls-nama-677", title: "Balipriyā", category: "Hṛdaya-rājñī", summary: "She who is especially fond of sacrificial offerings. Bali first means the daily morsel set aside for all creatures, the rite of universal feeding counted among the five great household sacrifices, and She loves its practitioners. Yet the commentary hears a second drumbeat: bali also means the strong, since inner enemies of sense and mind yield only to sturdy seekers, and the Upaniṣads demand vigorous body and resolute mind as liberty's prerequisites. She is equally fond of offerings made to Her attendant deities, all of whom serve under Her flag. In practice, feed some creature daily as Her own mouth, and toughen the instrument by one discipline of body and one of mind.", relatedVerseIds: ["ls-132"] },
  { id: "ls-nama-678", title: "Bhāṣārūpā", category: "Hṛdaya-rājñī", summary: "She who is in the form of language. Bhāṣā is all speech by which learning and understanding travel on sound, and the name makes Her its substance, the Sound-Brahman Herself. The commentary crystallizes the metaphysics in a wedding image: Śaktī is sound and Śiva is meaning, the two united like a word and its sense, for a word divorced from meaning is noise and a meaning without word never arrives. Every true sentence is therefore Their embrace. In practice, refuse empty chatter and careless reading today, uttering fewer words more heavily meant, and listen in each meaningful sentence for the divine Couple meeting on your tongue.", relatedVerseIds: ["ls-133"] },
  { id: "ls-nama-679", title: "Bṛhatsenā", category: "Hṛdaya-rājñī", summary: "She who has a vast army. Bṛhat-senā commands an innumerable host, the instrument by which the universe's administration is enforced across every quarter. The commentary bids us read this alongside the previous name's strong ones, suggesting Her soldiers are drawn from disciplined souls and shining powers rather than mere numbers, quality mustered into quantity. No disorder in creation goes unanswered for lack of troops. In practice, stop fighting your battles as a deserter and enlist, placing your small strength under Her banner each morning, and trust that the vast battalions stand behind your single faithful post. Even a lone sentry counts when the Queen inspects the line.", relatedVerseIds: ["ls-133"] },
  { id: "ls-nama-680", title: "Bhāvābhāvavivarjitā", category: "Hṛdaya-rājñī", summary: "She who is beyond being and non-being. Bhāva is existence verified by living experiment, while abhāva, non-existence woven by illusion, divides into the prior absence before a thing is made, the posterior absence after it perishes, the absolute absence of what never is, and the mutual absence of this-not-that. The commentary walks this fourfold ladder only to kick it away with the Upaniṣadic not this, not this, since the soul imagined different from Brahman differs only through ignorance. She stands past both affirmation and denial. In practice, practice one round of neti-neti, denying of yourself each label that arrives, neither clinging to existence nor romanticizing void, until the Denier Herself remains.", relatedVerseIds: ["ls-133"] },
  { id: "ls-nama-681", title: "Sukhārādhyā", category: "Hṛdaya-rājñī", summary: "She who is easily worshipped. Sukha-ārādhyā promises a worship without torment: no fasting unto ruin, no fire-walking, no mortification, since harming the body harms Her dwelling within it. The commentary turns the body into Her temple, the heart its sanctum and the indwelling Brahman its idol, and summons Kṛṣṇa's censure of unsanctioned self-torture as demonic resolve, while warning that ease is not indulgence. A healthy frame wedded to moderate meditation is the lawful vehicle of realization. In practice, abandon one needless austerity that punishes rather than purifies, keep one steady moderate discipline instead, and worship the Temple-dweller by maintaining Her temple in good repair.", relatedVerseIds: ["ls-133"] },
  { id: "ls-nama-682", title: "Śubhakarī", category: "Hṛdaya-rājñī", summary: "She who does good. Śubha-karī answers the anxious doubt that effortless worship might earn neglect or harm: the Vāc Devis placed this name to swear that She does only good for Her devotees, even when worshipped imperfectly. The commentary roots this in motherhood itself, for She is Śrī Mātā, and in marriage to the Auspicious One, so that a mere thought of Her already brings weal. True love, not flawless ritual, is the required offering. In practice, replace fear-driven bargaining with simple affection, turning to Her once today with no request attached, and watch how deliberate love draws answering goodness faster than anxious perfection ever did.", relatedVerseIds: ["ls-133"] },
  { id: "ls-nama-683", title: "Śobhanāsulabhāgatiḥ", category: "Hṛdaya-rājñī", summary: "She who is attained through a bright and easy path. The name dissects into śobhana the radiant goal, sulabhā its easy accessibility, and gati the road itself: worshipping Her is a luminous highway to liberation, not a thorny track. The commentary distinguishes heaven-sojourns, where merit rests before rebirth resumes, from final release where the soul merges with Brahman past all return, and notes the Vāc Devis ring changes on shining across three names, some dividers even reading this as two or three separate salutations. Brightness and ease coincide here. In practice, choose the clearer kinder duty over the ostentatiously severe one, and walk today's stretch of the shining road with the confidence that its Engineer designed it to be traveled.", relatedVerseIds: ["ls-133"] },
  { id: "ls-nama-684", title: "Rājarājeśvarī", category: "Hṛdaya-rājñī", summary: "She who is the ruler of kings and emperors. Rāja opens a septet of royal names: She is Empress over Brahmā, Viṣṇu, and Rudra the cosmic kings, and over Kubera the lord of wealth, worshipped even by him. The commentary garlands Her with Śaṅkara's portrait of the mantra-queen, sealed with hrīṁ at each mantra-peak, fond of inward worship, mothering all beings as family, breathed through the haṁsa chant, tender as Kāmākṣī compassion, and blissful in Her wheel-worship. Royalty here is sacramental, not merely political. In practice, bring your ruling ambitions, over households, teams, or your own unruly senses, under Her higher crown, and govern your small province as Her viceroy rather than its tyrant.", relatedVerseIds: ["ls-134"] },
  { id: "ls-nama-685", title: "Rājyadāyinī", category: "Hṛdaya-rājñī", summary: "She who gives dominion. Rājya-dāyinī allots souls their post-mortal territories, the radiant suburbs of Vaikuṇṭha or Kailāsa earned by ripe merit under Her universal administration. The commentary carefully bounds the gift: such residence is splendid tenancy rather than ownership, since exhausted merit returns the soul to birth, a sojourn distinct from final merger where no karmic balance, good or bad, remains to fund another journey. Thrones are consolation prizes beside union. In practice, long for Her rather than for Her heavens, doing today's good without calculating its celestial rent, and ask the Giver of dominions for the one territory from which no tenant is ever evicted.", relatedVerseIds: ["ls-134"] },
  { id: "ls-nama-686", title: "Rājyavallabhā", category: "Hṛdaya-rājñī", summary: "She who protects all the dominions. Vallabhā is the beloved guardian, and as queen of queens She shelters every realm She allots, confirming the previous grant with protection. The commentary relocates these kingdoms mystically into Śrīcakra itself, where Brahmā, Viṣṇu, Rudra, Indra, and all the shining hosts dwell as Her court, so that guarding dominions means keeping Her own luminous household in order. Her empire's provinces are chapels. In practice, commend your own small dominion of home and work to Her wardenship each morning, inspecting its borders with a guardian's care, and rest in the paradox of being both the guarded province and the guardian's child.", relatedVerseIds: ["ls-134"] },
  { id: "ls-nama-687", title: "Rājatkṛpā", category: "Hṛdaya-rājñī", summary: "She who has a compassion that captivates everyone. Rājat-kṛpā is shining mercy, compassion with a radiance that wins hearts the way light wins eyes. The commentary needs only one line because the doctrine is single: this glowing tenderness is the primary quality of Śrī Mātā, the root from which Her justice, generosity, and play all flower. Captivation is not coercion but dawn. In practice, expose one hardened corner of yourself to that sunrise today, naming the fault or grief you hide even from friends, and let Her shining compassion do what self-scolding never could, melting resistance into answering love. No heart stays unmoved where Her sunrise reaches.", relatedVerseIds: ["ls-134"] },
  { id: "ls-nama-688", title: "Rājapīṭhaniveśitanijāśritā", category: "Hṛdaya-rājñī", summary: "She who establishes on royal thrones of those who take refuge in Her. She seats surrendered devotees upon thrones, as Devī Māhātmya records of those who yielded to Her. Yet the commentary warns that the gift is also an examination: corporeal splendor tests whether the recipient can handle elevation without intoxication, and whoever passes through indifference to bait is promoted from furniture to family, granted a station in Her own domain as the gods themselves once were in the wheel. Thrones are sieves for souls. In practice, receive any advancement today as precisely such a test, enjoying it without clutching it, and prove readiness for Her domain by remaining inwardly unseated amid outer seating.", relatedVerseIds: ["ls-134"] },
  { id: "ls-nama-689", title: "Rājyalakṣmī", category: "Hṛdaya-rājñī", summary: "She who is the embodiment of the prosperity of the world. Rājya-lakṣmī is the fortune of the whole kingdom, and since Her kingdom is the entire universe with boundaries past human reckoning, its immeasurable wealth is Her own body. The commentary derives this from Her empress-title: only supreme authority can hold universal riches, so majesty and opulence coincide in one figure. She does not own wealth; She is what wealth means. In practice, handle money and plenty as fragments of Her person rather than private trophies, spending with the dignity of a treasurer and sharing with the freedom of one who knows the Treasury is inexhaustible.", relatedVerseIds: ["ls-135"] },
  { id: "ls-nama-690", title: "Kośanāthā", category: "Hṛdaya-rājñī", summary: "She who is the mistress of the treasury. Kośa is the state reserve, and commanding many kingdoms She naturally keeps their consolidated strongroom. The commentary opens the word's second vault: the five sheaths veiling the soul, food, breath, mind, understanding, and bliss, already mapped in an earlier name, with the self-lit Self glowing beneath them all, and declares Her that innermost gold. She guards both the empire's bullion and the heart's. In practice, inventory your five coverings in meditation, thanking each sheath for its service while refusing to mistake it for yourself, and ask the Mistress of the treasury to display the Coin she keeps wrapped inside.", relatedVerseIds: ["ls-135"] },
  { id: "ls-nama-691", title: "Caturaṅgabaleśvarī", category: "Hṛdaya-rājñī", summary: "She who commands armies of four types. Catur-aṅga is the classic fourfold host of horse, elephant, chariot, and foot, and She is its supreme commander in the outer field of cosmic order. The commentary turns the formation inward as well, mustering mind, intellect, conscience, and ego as the inner army whose power, undisciplined, can wreck mayhem within a single breast. Either way She rules the troops, the general of galaxies and of the ganglia. In practice, inspect your inner divisions daily, posting mind to its watch, intellect to counsel, conscience to judgment, and ego to baggage-duty, and report the whole formation each morning to its Commander-in-chief.", relatedVerseIds: ["ls-135"] },
  { id: "ls-nama-692", title: "Sāmrājyadāyinī", category: "Hṛdaya-rājñī", summary: "She who is the bestower of imperial dominion. Sāmrājya outranks rājya as empire outranks kingdom: if the realms of Brahmā, Viṣṇu, and Śiva are provinces, Her domain of Śrīcakra, where sages chant between its inner ramparts, is the imperial capital She grants to devoted worshippers. The commentary crowns the politics with metaphysics, for emperors once proved rank through the Rājasūya sacrifice, while Taittirīya reserves true self-rule for whoever realizes identity with Brahman, making the Self-realized the only genuine monarchs. Empire here is enlightenment with territory. In practice, stop campaigning for larger worldly jurisdiction and qualify for Hers, letting each act of dedication annex one more village of the heart to Her capital.", relatedVerseIds: ["ls-135"] },
  { id: "ls-nama-693", title: "Satyasandhā", category: "Hṛdaya-rājñī", summary: "She who is devoted to (or maintains) truth. Satya-sandhā is bound by truth, determining every cosmic case upon it alone, Her vows undefeated by any force because reality itself backs them. The commentary hears Chāndogya's lover of truth and one whose every resolve is truth, and the Bhāgavata's prayer to Her as prompter of truthful speech and undifferentiating vision, noting Viṣṇu bears the same seal. As Śrī Mātā She has vowed the universe's sustenance, and truth is the vow's spine. In practice, make one resolution today in Her style, small, true, and unbreakable, keeping it exactly as spoken, and feel how fidelity in miniature aligns you with the Fidelity that upholds worlds.", relatedVerseIds: ["ls-135"] },
  { id: "ls-nama-694", title: "Sāgaramekhalā", category: "Hṛdaya-rājñī", summary: "She who is girdled by the oceans. Mekhalā is a woman's girdle, and Hers is stitched of oceans, the planet's vast waters circling Her waist like a sash. The commentary reads this as the portrait of bigger-than-the-biggest from Kaṭha, complementing Her smaller-than-small subtlety: every sized being flows from the Supreme Self, and earth itself with all its seas becomes one ornament on Her immeasurable form. With this image the royal septet that began at the Empress-name closes its circle. In practice, stand before any large water today, even a rain-filled basin, and see it as a thread of Her girdle, letting Her vastness re-size your anxieties to their proper smallness.", relatedVerseIds: ["ls-135"] },
  { id: "ls-nama-695", title: "Dīkṣitā", category: "Hṛdaya-rājñī", summary: "She who is under a vow. Dīkṣā is initiation, mantra first and then the bliss-surnamed consecration-name at the guru's discretion, matched to the disciple's ripeness for Brahman-knowledge. The commentary enthrones Her as both giver and substance of this rite: as Supreme Guru already hailed, She burns away the candidate's sins in compassion, while the tradition quietly admits the tension with karma's inviolable law, and resolves it in non-difference, guru, mantra, and deity being one entity in Her school. The initiator is the Initiated. In practice, renew your own initiation by keeping one vow scrupulously today, and approach your teacher, your mantra, and your goddess as three doors into the same sanctum.", relatedVerseIds: ["ls-136"] },
  { id: "ls-nama-696", title: "Daityaśamanī", category: "Hṛdaya-rājñī", summary: "She who destroys the demons, wicked forces. Daitya-śamanī slays not storybook giants but the interior branch of evil, anger, lust, and their kin operating within to blockade realization. The commentary adds the swiftest weapon in Her armory: the very thought of Her can rout mental adversities, since darkness cannot hold ground where light is merely remembered. Destruction here is rescue, the battlefield a mercy. In practice, the moment an affliction rises, speak Her name once instead of negotiating with the invader, and let that single remembrance be the sword-stroke that beheads the demon before it fortifies its camp. Repeat until the mind rests clear.", relatedVerseIds: ["ls-136"] },
  { id: "ls-nama-697", title: "Sarvalokavaśaṅkarī", category: "Hṛdaya-rājñī", summary: "She who keeps all the worlds under Her control. Vaśaṁ-karī brings every world under Her sway, whether counted as the Gāyatrī's three or the greater litany's seven from earth up through intermediate heavens to truth's own abode. The commentary reads these altitudes as grades of consciousness, so that subjugation means the Supreme administration extends from matter to the threshold of Brahman, and the climber's ascent from lowest earth to highest truth travels entirely within Her jurisdiction. No level is ungoverned. In practice, locate today's altitude honestly, earth-bound worry or halfway aspiration, and offer that level's business to its Governor, climbing one deliberate rung toward truth under Her escort.", relatedVerseIds: ["ls-136"] },
  { id: "ls-nama-698", title: "Sarvārthadātrī", category: "Hṛdaya-rājñī", summary: "She who grants all desires. Artha here spans the fourfold aims of life, righteousness, wealth, love, and liberation, the complete curriculum of what humans may rightly seek. The commentary, as befits the Empress and Mother in one, blesses all four while ranking their pursuit: the Vedas crown duty and release as supreme, while wealth and desire turn benevolent or dangerous according to whether they serve shared flourishing or private passion. She provides the whole syllabus but grades the motive. In practice, audit one current desire against the fourfold scale, purifying its manner of pursuit, and ask the Grantor to fulfill it in the form most compatible with righteousness and release.", relatedVerseIds: ["ls-136"] },
  { id: "ls-nama-699", title: "Sāvitrī", category: "Hṛdaya-rājñī", summary: "She who is the creative power in the universe. Sāvitrī is the twenty-four-syllabled Gāyatrī herself, creation's daylight prayer, and the feminine of Savitā the Creator, a title worn by Śiva, the sun, fire, wind, and waters in the Sāvitrī Upaniṣad's chain of begetting pairs. The commentary reads every such pair as Śiva and Śaktī in working dress, making Her the Creator in person rather than His assistant, and adds that all gods worship Her precisely for this stainless generativity, purity of thought and act shining visibly upon the face. In practice, greet the morning sun as Her altar-lamp, recite the Gāyatrī as though returning borrowed light, and let one clean thought and one clean act today prove your share in Her creativeness.", relatedVerseIds: ["ls-136"] },
  { id: "ls-nama-700", title: "Saccidānandarūpiṇī", category: "Hṛdaya-rājñī", summary: "She who is of the nature of existence, consciousness and bliss. Sat, cit, and ānanda are not parts assembled into Brahman, which has no parts, but its threefold glow as seen through illusion's prism: first the seeker verifies that the Real exists, then clarifies awareness to higher planes, and finally drowns in bliss, stages successive in us though eternally one in it. The commentary closes the vast royal cycle by re-certifying Her Absolute status in this most famous of formulas, existence that cannot be negated, consciousness that cannot be objectified, bliss that cannot expire. In practice, rehearse the triad inwardly, affirming I am, noticing I know, resting I rejoice, until the three sentences converge into Her single name.", relatedVerseIds: ["ls-136"] },
];
